 - Added `/tui fullscreen`, a Claude Code-style alternate-screen surface for the live primary transcript: the editor and a hint/status footer stay pinned at the bottom while the transcript scrolls above them with `PgUp`/`PgDn`, `Shift+PgUp`/`Shift+PgDn` (jump prompt to prompt), `Ctrl+Home`/`Ctrl+End`, and the mouse wheel. A sticky header names the user prompt whose section is on screen and snaps back to it when clicked, the viewport stays anchored to that prompt while blocks above it keep streaming, and the footer reports how many rows arrived since auto-follow paused. The surface keeps the rest of the interactive chrome alive between the transcript and the editor — working loader, token/cost readout, todo and subagent HUDs, error banners, pending messages, and hook widgets — so a streaming turn looks the same as it does in the default renderer, and it reserves the scrollbar column at all times so crossing one screenful never reflows the transcript by a column. `/tui default` (or `/tui close`) restores the native-scrollback renderer and `/tui status` reports the active mode; the preference applies to the current session only.
 - Fixed fullscreen transcript wheel scrolling discarding Ghostty's accelerated burst: each native SGR wheel report now retains the terminal's default three-row discrete delta instead of being reduced to one row when reports arrive within the same burst, and each report opens a display-rate render window so a fast spin paints one frame per report instead of landing as a few large jumps. Measured over a 450 ms gesture of 50 native reports: 14 paints moving a median of 11 rows each before, 49 paints moving a median of 3 rows each after — the smoothness costs ~3.5x the bytes for the duration of the gesture, bounded by the alt-screen per-line diff and the engine's adaptive backpressure.
 - Added usage telemetry and an automatic lifecycle for auto-learn managed skills. Injecting an `omp-managed` skill into a turn (slash command, ACP, RPC, or autoload) now records a use count and timestamp in `~/.omp/agent/managed-skills/usage.json`; discovery alone never counts, and authored skills are never recorded. When `autolearn.enabled` is on, each top-level session start runs one non-blocking curation pass: a skill is only ever judged on a pass after the one that first observed it, a never-used skill inside the stale window is left alone, and otherwise `lastUsedAt ?? createdAt` older than `autolearn.curator.archiveAfterDays` (default 90) moves the skill to `managed-skills/.archive/` — out of discovery, reversible with one move — while older than `autolearn.curator.staleAfterDays` (default 30) only flags it. Using a stale skill again reactivates it. Passes that change something append one developer message naming what moved.
+- Added `session_search`, a tool for recalling past conversations from their message content. Session messages are indexed incrementally into a local SQLite FTS5 index as they are persisted, and the tool infers its mode from the arguments given: a `query` ranks matching sessions by BM25 with one hit per session plus surrounding context, a `sessionId` with `aroundEntryId` scrolls a window in either direction from that anchor, a `sessionId` alone returns the session's first and last messages, and no arguments lists recent sessions. No model call is involved on any path. Indexing covers user and assistant messages of persisted sessions from the point the feature is enabled — there is no backfill of prior sessions — and `sessionSearch.enabled` (default `true`) gates both the index and the tool.
 
